   "summary": "The text traces the historical development of democracy in the United States and France, highlighting popular sovereignty and constitutional evolution. It analyzes three principal forms of democratic government—the British parliamentary system, American separation of powers, and Swiss direct democracy—contrasting them with autocracies and dictatorships. It then reviews the progressive expansion of the suffrage through the abolition of tax and property restrictions and the granting of equal voting rights to women, focusing on Japan's political modernization. Finally, the text explains the function of majority rule in democratic decision-making, emphasizing that freedom of speech, open discussion, and protection of minority viewpoints are vital safeguards against tyranny.",
   "title": "Primer of Democracy (Chapters II–V)",
